<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # A Tall Tree! – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Our Garden. <b>Theme:</b> A Tall Tree!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Match simple words to corresponding pictures. Reading: Identify simple words for very common symbols. Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can match nature-related words with primary or secondary colors to corresponding pictures (e.g., "pink flowers," "green tree," "yellow leaves"). Can recognize written words for nature symbols when supported by pictures (e.g., leaves, seeds). Can recognize sounds of letters related to nature-related vocabulary when supported with visuals (e.g., identifying the /s/ sound in "sun" when shown a picture of the sun or the /t/ sound in "tree" when shown an image of a tree).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage aims to engage students, activate prior knowledge, and set the context for the lesson through interactive activities and clear demonstrations.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and starts with a "My Garden" song and actions (TPR). The teacher shows large flashcards of "garden," "tree," "flower," "sun," "seed." Students chant the words and make simple actions (e.g., pretend to water a plant for "grow," stretch arms up for "tall tree"). The teacher points to a flashcard of a "tree" and says, "This is a tree. A tall tree!" emphasizing the /t/ sound.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a tree and writes the word "tree" on the board next to it, saying the word clearly. Then, the teacher holds up a picture of a "garden" and writes "garden" on the board, emphasizing the /g/ sound. The teacher models matching the word "tree" to the picture of a tree, then the word "garden" to the picture of a garden. The teacher also models reading simple sentences like "The tree is tall." pointing to each word and picture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word "flower" and then to a picture of a flower) "Is this a flower?" (Students nod or say "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word "tree" and then to a picture of a sun) "Is this a tree?" (Students shake heads or say "No!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">New language and content are presented, connected to the scenario, using authentic or semi-authentic materials. Comprehension tasks ensure students grasp the main idea.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario "It's Our Garden.":</b> The teacher displays a large poster titled "Our Garden Story" with 4-5 simple sentences, each accompanied by a clear picture. The text uses target vocabulary and grammar:
                    <br>"This is my garden." (Picture of a garden)
                    <br>"This is a tall tree." (Picture of a tall tree)
                    <br>"The flowers are pink." (Picture of pink flowers)
                    <br>"A small seed grows." (Picture of a seed growing into a sprout)
                    <br>"The sun is big." (Picture of a big sun)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short descriptive text with pictures (Poster/Storybook page).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Recognizing basic information in short, simple written texts supported by visuals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher points to the poster and asks, "What is this story about?" (Guide students to say "Garden!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Visual Check):</b> The teacher reads a sentence, then makes a statement about the picture. "The flowers are pink." (Points to pink flowers). "Is this true?" (Students say "True!" or give a thumbs up). "The tree is short." (Points to the tall tree). "Is this true?" (Students say "False!" or give a thumbs down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> The teacher points to the word "tree" on the poster and asks, "Where is the tree?" Students point to the corresponding picture. Repeat for "flowers," "seed," and "sun."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on developing accuracy in reading, practicing the target grammar and vocabulary through guided activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading: Matching words to pictures, identifying words for symbols. Grammar: Noun + verb + adjective ("The flowers are pink."), Present simple ("This is a tall tree."). Vocabulary: garden, tree, flower, seed, sun, pink, tall, big, grow, is. Phonemic awareness: /s/ in "sun", /t/ in "tree".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Picture-Word Match Worksheet.</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of a "tree," "flower," "seed," and "sun" on one side and a scrambled list of the words "flower," "sun," "tree," "seed" on the other. Students draw a line from each word to its matching picture. The teacher circulates, offering support and asking, "What word is this?" for reinforcement.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice: Sentence-Picture Completion.</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with simple sentences and blank spaces, each accompanied by a picture. Students choose the correct word from a provided word bank (e.g., "tall," "pink," "big") to complete each sentence. For example: "The tree is ______." (picture of a tall tree); "The flowers are ______." (picture of pink flowers). Students read the completed sentences aloud chorally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check: "Read and Show."</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair gets a set of flashcards with target vocabulary words (e.g., "garden," "tree," "flower," "seed," "sun") and corresponding picture cards. Student A picks a word card and reads it aloud. Student B finds the matching picture card and shows it. Then they swap roles. The teacher monitors pronunciation, especially /g/ in "garden" and /t/ in "tree," and provides immediate feedback.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the language in a meaningful and realistic communicative task, focusing on fluency and interaction within the lesson's scenario and theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: "My Garden Labels."</b> Students create small labels for a class "mini-garden" display (pictures of plants, flowers, trees, sun, seeds). Each student receives a set of pre-printed words (e.g., "tree," "flower," "leaf," "seed," "sun," "garden") and a sheet of paper with different garden pictures. Students cut out the words and glue each word next to its corresponding picture. They can also draw a simple picture for words like "my garden" or "tall tree" if not provided. This activity integrates reading and simple identification, reinforcing the connection between written words and visual representations in the context of "It's Our Garden."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual word-to-picture labeling activity, contributing to a class display.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme "A Tall Tree!" and Scenario "It's Our Garden.":</b> Students are actively reading and labeling elements of their garden, including "tree," "flower," and "seed," reinforcing "The tree is tall" and "The flowers are pink."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Reading fluency by recognizing target words and matching them to pictures, successful completion of the labeling task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage involves formative assessment to check learning outcomes and allows students to reflect on their own progress.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "My Garden Labels" activity and a quick "show me" assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher holds up a picture (e.g., a pink flower). Students must find and hold up the word card "pink flower" from their previous activity or write the word "flower" on a mini-whiteboard/paper. Alternatively, the teacher says a word (e.g., "tree"), and students point to the correct picture on their worksheet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in matching words to pictures (e.g., "pink flowers," "green tree").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognition of written words for nature symbols (e.g., "leaves," "seeds").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempted identification of sounds of letters in nature-related vocabulary (e.g., /s/ in "sun" if asked to read aloud).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a small drawing of a smiley face, a neutral face, and a sad face. The teacher asks, "Can you read the words for our garden?" Students circle the face that shows how they feel about reading the words today. (Smiley for 'Yes, I can!', Neutral for 'A little bit', Sad for 'Not yet').</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage encourages both student and teacher to reflect on the lesson's effectiveness and plan for future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you read today?" (Teacher shows picture cards and students identify the words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you like to read about in our garden?" (Students might point to a specific picture or word).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., the visual matching activities, peer reading).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (e.g., more phonetic practice for specific sounds, simpler sentences for some students)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students engaged? Did they achieve the learning outcome of matching words to pictures and recognizing symbols?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction):</b> In the next lesson, students will actively speak and interact using the vocabulary and simple sentences they practiced reading today. We will use the words from our "My Garden Labels" activity to describe what we see and ask simple questions about the garden with their classmates.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>